    "explanation": "Both of you are partway right, and the route-deviation rule sends this one to a human reviewer rather than settling it here. What the record establishes: the trip deviated 1.40 km from the recommended route (14.80 km recorded actual minus 13.40 km recommended, EV-TRIP-06007), which is over the 1.0 km tolerance in POL-RD-22. The LTA advisory (EV-ADVIS-06007-01) records an ACCIDENT on the PIE towards Tuas after the Adam Road exit, valid 08:35-09:48, which overlaps the trip window of 08:48-09:16; that stretch is on the recommended PIE leg in the direction of travel, so the reroute was traffic-justified under POL-RD-23. I therefore reject the rider's argument that POL-RD-23 fails because the derived data left 'on the recommended route' undetermined: the advisory's road, direction and location resolve that question in the driver's favour. The driver's 09:00:37 in-app message ('Sir, accident on PIE after Adam Road, I exit Adam Rd and go Holland Road, ok?') was sent before the platform logged the deviation at 09:03:37, so the notice duty in POL-RD-21 was met. But the rider expressly refused within the minute ('Please don't, stay on PIE' at 09:01:12, read at 09:01:21), so the deviation was not agreed. That combination - traffic-justified, driver informed, rider objected, deviation over 1 km - fits none of the five rows of POL-RD-24: row (a) needs the rider's agreement, row (b) needs the rider to have been uninformed, and rows (c) and (e) need a deviation of 1 km or less. POL-RD-24 says in terms that a case fitting no row, 'for example, informed but the rider objected', is escalated under POL-GEN-26, and that is exactly this case; the rider's alternative that rows (b) or (d) keep the money with the rider fails on their own predicates. So the case goes to a human reviewer. The money in issue is the excess distance fare: 1.40 km x 0.72 SGD per km = 1.01 SGD without surge, which is the 1.01 SGD the fare ledger (EV-FARE-06007) records as already refunded to the rider and recovered from the driver at 10:33:23. On escalation no monetary outcome issues and that amount is held rather than collected or refunded, so neither the driver's claim to have it back nor a final refund to the rider is decided here. Confidence is 0.90: every artifact carrying these facts (trip record, chat log, traffic advisory, fare ledger, GPS trace) sits at full weight 1.0. No referrals are added; a route deviation has no referral clause in this version."
